    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d853">

                <pb facs="0931" n="645" xml:id="pg645"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 341.622a/134</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The German Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bernstorff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">J. Nr. A 3485</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-05-13"><hi rendition="#italic">May 13,
                                1916</hi></date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received May
                        16</hi>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary of State</hi>: In reply to your kind
                    note No. 1919<anchor n="1" xml:id="d853-f1"/> of the 4th of this month I have
                    the honor to say that the German subjects taken from the S. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">China</hi> do not belong to the German military
                    organization.</p>
                <p>The German subject Streckfuss, to be sure, was a temporary member of the guard of
                    the Imperial Legation at Peking and the German subject E. Klein was for a while
                    employed as unarmed watchman in the city of Tsingtau, but both had been for some
                    time before discharged from any military service liability. They are
                    non-combatants arid both being over 48 years old are no longer subject to
                    military duty under the law of Germany.</p>
                <p>So far as known, the Austro-Hungarian and Turkish subjects taken from the ship
                    are likewise civilians. Yet I would leave it with your excellency to apply to
                    Austro-Hungarian and Turkish representatives on their account.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">J. Bernstorff</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
